# Notebooks — Les Hyperion

This directory contains all Jupyter notebooks for the Les Hyperion portfolio. The site reads `index.json` to discover and display notebooks automatically.

---

## Adding a New Notebook

### Step 1 — Create your notebook

Write your analysis in a Jupyter notebook (`.ipynb`). Save it in this `notebooks/` folder.

### Step 2 — Register it in `index.json`

Open `notebooks/index.json` and add an entry following this format:

```json
{
  "file":     "My_Analysis.ipynb",
  "category": "Remote Sensing",
  "title":    "My Analysis Title",
  "desc":     "A short description shown on the portfolio card.",
  "keywords": ["keyword1", "keyword2", "keyword3"],
  "date":     "2026-02-21",
  "lang":     "R"
}
```

| Field | Required | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `file` | ✅ | Filename including `.ipynb` extension |
| `category` | ✅ | One of: `GIS`, `Remote Sensing`, `Biodiversity`, `Biostatistics`, `Research` |
| `title` | ✅ | Display title on the portfolio card |
| `desc` | ✅ | One-sentence description (shown on card) |
| `keywords` | ✅ | Array of 2–5 keyword strings |
| `date` | ✅ | ISO 8601 format: `YYYY-MM-DD` |
| `lang` | ✅ | Primary language: `R` or `Python` |

### Step 3 — Commit and push

```bash
git add notebooks/My_Analysis.ipynb notebooks/index.json
git commit -m "Add My Analysis notebook"
git push
```

The portfolio site will automatically pick up the new notebook.

---

## Notebook Style Guide

To ensure your notebooks render well on the portfolio site, follow these conventions:

**Markdown cells:**
- Use `#` for top-level section headings (rendered as large headings in the viewer)
- Use `##` for sub-headings (rendered in accent colour)
- Keep paragraphs concise — the viewer shows the first 1000 characters per cell

**Code cells:**
- Include meaningful comments
- Print informative output (e.g., `cat(...)` in R, `print(...)` in Python) — the first output line is used as the card preview

**Cell order:**
- Start with a markdown title cell (`# My Title`)
- Follow with an abstract or introduction paragraph
- Then code → output → explanation

